From 44a25c7e0844190603296c876e6ccdea1cca62db Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: lccc-agent <agent@arena.invalid>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 01:22:23 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 01/17] x86: ISA permissions from GCC 16.2's measured -march/-m
 table

Every -march= name and -m ISA option now goes through one table that
scripts/gen_x86_arch_table.py generates from GCC 16.2. For each CPU it
records the feature set, the macro-only extensions and the CPU-name
macros. For each option it records the set, general-regs-only and no-
masks, measured from a fully denied base, plus GCC's override
implications. Before writing, the generator checks the driver's
-march=CPU -mno-N model against GCC for every CPU, mode and negatable
option (8772 cases). --check re-measures GCC and fails on any
difference. The hand-kept profiles and implications this replaces had
drifted:
- Sapphire Rapids without AVX-VNNI and AMX;
- AVX-512 subsets kept under -mno-avx2;
- no PCLMUL for -mvpclmulqdq.

target("...") follows GCC: options apply in source order, arch=CPU
replaces the features (and forgets explicit -m grants, as GCC does),
and errors use GCC's wording. One fold, function_feature_bits, feeds
code generation, intrinsic permission, the inliner and the vector
passes. A callee is inlined only if its features are a subset of the
caller's. Header intrinsics (bmi2intrin.h, popcntintrin.h) check
permission where they are used.

check_x86_march_macros.py (204 cases) and check_x86_mflag_macros.py (714
cases) compare predefined macros with GCC 16.2. The intrinsic-feature,
target-attribute and scalar-intrinsic gates gain legs for the new rules.
docs/x86-isa-permissions-and-simd-abi.md documents the layers. It
replaces the session note docs/SESSION_FOLLOWUP_S144_REBASE_979.md.
