From cc52f3a2dded812d4140f78ac8703c209d5d2d56 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: lccc-agent <agent@arena.invalid>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 01:48:29 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 02/17] x86: -mtune vocabulary, frame-pointer omission with
 balanced %rsp moves

-mtune= accepts exactly GCC 16.2's vocabulary for the mode: the -march=
names without x86-64-v2/v3/v4, plus generic and intel. It is measured
at -m64 and -m32 over 130 names and generated into arch_table.rs. GCC
rejects any other name with "bad value for '-mtune=' switch", and
configure and Kbuild cc-option probes depend on that rejection. Every
accepted name resolves to a tuning row, the closest modelled core or
Generic. -mapxf is refused for 32-bit code, as in GCC.

Dead callee-saved pushes are removed even when the body moves %rsp
temporarily. Upstream's dynamic_rsp_write gate kept them in that case.
Removal is safe when the moves are balanced:
- for an SSE1 scalar GPR/XMM bridge, the frame-preserving strategy grows
  the subq/addq by the push's 8 bytes;
- call-argument pushes are cleaned up after the call.
Either way %rsp at every body instruction is unchanged, so every
%rsp-relative slot and the call's 16-byte alignment are preserved. CFI
is synthesised afterwards, so -funwind-tables does not change the input
to this pass.
