Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 15:21:36 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 05/17] x86: __builtin_cpu_is with GCC 16.2's processor names

Accept every vendor, type and subtype name GCC 16.2 folds (73, generated
from libgcc's processor tables by scripts/gen_x86_cpu_is_table.py, which
CI runs with --check) and lower each to one comparison against libgcc's
__cpu_model at the offsets GCC uses (+0 vendor, +4 type, +8 subtype).
Unknown names and non-literal arguments get GCC's diagnostics.

A function that reads __cpu_model calls __cpu_indicator_init behind a
guard first: libgcc initialises the model from a priority-101
constructor, which runs unordered with a user's priority-101
constructor, so an unguarded read there sees zeros. The call is on a
cold path; GCC omits the guard and has the race.

Gate: tests/regression/check_cpu_is_runtime.sh (17/17), which compares
each name's runtime result with GCC 16.2's on the host.
