From a75882afe675d0b054d3eb8eb61aeffa683a5bd7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: lccc-agent <agent@arena.invalid>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 15:21:36 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 06/17] DWARF: entry rows and compile units; -g no longer
 changes code

Under -g every function now gets a DWARF 4 compile unit and an entry row
at the body's opening brace, placed before the prologue as GCC does, so
'break func' stops after the prologue and the frames resolve to file:line.

-g used to change the generated code: x86-64 36/29/19 files differed at
-O1/-O2/-O3, i686 26/54/60. The cause was .loc rows inside peephole
match windows. The rows are now hidden from the matchers as no-ops and
emitted again afterwards. The opening row is inserted after the
peephole and CFI synthesis. The window fixes this needed:
- dead_stores counted the hidden rows against its window;
- fp_const_hoist reused slots across them;
- i686 else_hoist_diamonds was blind to them.
pow2_cmov (#985) now asks FileLiveness instead of assuming every
register live: -O2 corpus asm is byte-identical to the pre-#985 output
again, where it had grown by 108 instructions.

Gate: tests/regression/check_debug_info_invariance.sh. It compiles the
corpus with and without -g and requires identical code modulo debug rows
(292 compilations; it fails on the previous compiler with 84 x86-64 and
140 i686 differences).
