From ca2a75d993311a8aa32c5dea0904bc80fcf273d2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: lccc-agent <agent@arena.invalid>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 15:21:49 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 07/17] linkers: compressed debug input, i686 section headers
 and debug sections

Compressed debug input: SHF_COMPRESSED (zlib and zstd Chdr) and .zdebug
sections were copied as raw bytes. The output then had compressed bytes
behind uncompressed headers, and the i686 linker refused the input.
This is the root cause of the #985 DWARF gate failure; #985 diagnosed
it as a parser strictness bug. New in-tree inflate and zstd decoders
(linker_common) make such links byte-identical to links of the
uncompressed objects (harness 1386/1386). Callgrind, 4 MB input:
inflate 110 M Ir (zlib 75 M), zstd 121.9 M (libzstd 69 M).

The i686 executable writer gains:
- a complete section-header table;
- .symtab/.strtab;
- debug sections, relocated statically with strict relocation checks.
It also folds REL addends: rows, low_pc and stmt_list used to lose
their implicit addends. gdb now resolves file:line in -m32 programs.

Other fixes:
- PROVIDE-style symbols (end, edata, __data_start) no longer override
  input definitions, as in GNU ld;
- COPY slots keep the library's alignment (they were 4 on i686 and 8 on
  x86-64, so a 16-byte vector faulted in movaps);
- -S/--strip-debug is honoured, where it used to be swallowed.

Tests: the i386 linker cases i386_section_headers_and_debug,
i386_provided_symbols_yield_to_inputs and i386_copy_relocation_alignment,
copy_reloc_keeps_library_alignment, and new legs in the DWARF gate
(executable/shared links on both ABIs, compressed input, strip-all and
strip-debug).
