Subject: [PATCH 11/17] linkers: GNU ld's --warn-common, common-vs-DSO binding
 and positional --as-needed

All four built-in linkers (x86-64, i686, AArch64, RISC-V) and standalone
lccc-ld now follow GNU ld 2.47, measured with bfd, lld and mold:

- --warn-common: bfd's two messages, chosen by input order
  (``common of `s' overridden by definition from L`` and
  ``definition of `s' overriding common from O``). Weak definitions stay
  silent. --fatal-warnings makes them errors.
- A common binds to a strong data definition in a shared library, in either
  order: COPY on x86-64, GLOB_DAT on i686. Weak, function, hidden or
  protected commons stay common; TLS never binds. lld keeps the common;
  mold and bfd bind.
- --as-needed is decided at the library's position, before binding. Only
  non-weak references and common takeovers make a library needed. Weak
  references never pull in a library or extract an archive member.
- .so operands are positional on every target. A library given before its
  first user is not needed, and an archive given before every reference is
  never extracted. i686 walks inputs positionally and keeps a monotone
  fallback, accepting orders bfd rejects (lld accepts them too).
- DT_NEEDED for soname-less libraries: as given for an operand;
  libfoo.so for -lfoo, -l: and INPUT(-lfoo).
- lccc-ld: -m emulations, -EL (no-op), -EB (error), --sysroot, and the
  single-dash -hash-style spelling.

Pre-existing bugs fixed along the way: a dead load_file_elf64; RISC-V
DT_NEEDED, weak extraction and positional .so; i686 order-blind binding
and group pre-emption; an fdopen probe; and a fixture that encoded
lccc-ld's old leniency.

Tests: _warn_common_fatal_order_test, _common_binds_shared_data_test,
_positional_shared_operands_test (incl. weak-ref-lib,
archive-before-user-lib and 6 cross cases), resolver and DT_NEEDED unit
tests. Linker suite 321 pass / 0 fail / 0 skip (oracles bfd, lld, mold).
