From ccc934315cc070bc58ca6dc7e385e828b795fe77 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: lccc-agent <agent@arena.invalid>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 20:36:12 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 12/17] assemblers: AArch64/RISC-V line tables; AArch64 literal
 pools wait for .ltorg

The AArch64 and RISC-V built-in assemblers ignored .file/.loc, so
`lccc-arm -g` and `lccc-riscv -g` objects had no line table at all.
gdb-multiarch could not set file:line breakpoints.

Both now build .debug_line from .file/.loc, plus the compile unit and
subprograms GAS synthesizes (producer "LCCC <version>"). The builders are
shared with x86 in elf/dwarf.rs. RISC-V linker relaxation deletes code
bytes, so under relaxation every code address (row changes, sequence
ends, low_pc/high_pc) is a relocation against a label (DW_FORM_addr
high_pc). A section+offset or a constant delta would go stale. Inputs
that carry their own .debug_line are left alone.

AArch64 miscompile fixed: every ignored directive (.loc, .arch, .ident,
...) flushed the pending literal pool. `ldr x0, =imm; .loc ...; add`
placed the constant between the ldr and the add, and the CPU executed it
(SIGILL). GAS flushes only at .ltorg/.pool, at a section change and at
the end of input. Now only .ltorg/.pool are pool directives.

x86: .file names are unescaped (GAS), and a malformed .loc is an error.

Evidence: decoded rows are identical to GNU as on the same .s, for the
objects and after a GNU ld link, at -O0 and -O2, including RISC-V
relaxation. Under qemu, gdb-multiarch 16.3 resolves file:line
breakpoints and backtraces across objects on both targets.

Tests: check_dwarf_line_tables.py gains the AArch64/RISC-V legs (rows
compared by instruction ordinal, so encoding size cannot mask a
difference; a SKIP line without cross binutils). It fails on the
previous binaries. New unit tests:
line_directives_do_not_dump_the_literal_pool,
loc_rows_become_a_line_table,
relaxable_code_is_addressed_through_labels,
norelax_code_is_addressed_by_section_offsets.
