From 46b5641a351abfa91aa11ea094dc41388f1043d8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: lccc-agent <agent@arena.invalid>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 22:17:42 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 13/17] RISC-V assembler: GNU as RVC compression and branch
 relaxation

The built-in assembler never compressed: .text of dbg2.c/far.c was
492/204 bytes against GNU as 2.47's 360/162 (+27%). Three more defects
surfaced while matching GNU as:
- rv32 `li` emitted `addiw`, an rv64-only instruction;
- a conditional branch to an undefined, weak or out-of-range target
  was a bare R_RISCV_BRANCH (+-4 KiB), so the link failed;
- `unimp` was rejected.

Compression follows GNU as per source mnemonic, not per instruction
word. Word-level compression over-compresses `mv rd,x0`,
`jalr rd,0(rs)` and `jal ra,L`, which GNU as keeps 4 bytes long. `li`
follows `load_const` (the C aliases see the raw operand;
`addiw rd,x0,imm` inside the recursion stays 4 bytes). Branches relax
monotonically to 2/4/6/8 bytes by GNU as's `relaxed_branch_length`.
Relocated instructions other than branches are never compressed.

Gate check_riscv_rvc_parity.py: rv64 18,491 and rv32 18,347 source lines
(field boundaries, a seeded random mix, branch reach), with .text and
relocations byte-identical to GNU as 2.47. The pre-RVC binary and a
word-level compressor both fail it, and it skips without riscv64
binutils. It runs in fast local CI and the backend job, 0.5 s.

The five elf_writer.rs unit tests that encoded uncompressed output were
re-derived against GNU as rv64gc: `-march=rv64imafdc`, because Debian's
bare `as` defaults to rv64g.
