From: lccc-agent <agent@arena.invalid>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 22:17:53 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 14/17] DWARF: rows for return values, closing braces and loop
 steps

At -O0 the code fetching a `return` operand, the epilogue and a `for`
loop's step carried no line row: `break dbg2.c:7` (a `return`) set no
breakpoint, and `next` skipped from the loop body straight to the next
statement. GCC 16.2 rows each of them.

Terminators now record the span of the statement that produced them, in
a per-function side table keyed by block label. The table is not stored
in Terminator or BasicBlock, which have hundreds of literal construction
sites. A recorded span is used only while the block's terminator still
has the recorded shape: block merges and edge retargeting change it, and
a stale span would describe the wrong code. The epilogue takes the
function body's closing-brace row.

Line 7 of dbg2.c went missing even with spans recorded: phi elimination
inserted trampolines and renumber_module_labels relabelled every block
without updating the table. Every relabelling pass now remaps it
(`TerminatorSpans::remap`/`retarget`).

Rows, x86-64 -O0 dbg2.c: lccc `3 4 5 6 7 8 11 12 13 14 13 15 16 17`,
GCC `3 4 5 6 7 8 11 12 13 13 14 14 13 13 15 16 16 17`. The difference
is GCC's second row for another column on the same line, which gdb's
stepping ignores. gdb stops at dbg2.c:7 on x86-64, AArch64 and RISC-V
(QEMU). On the cross targets `next` from line 6 lands on line 7.
